Paramagnetic species contains unpaired electrons in their molecular orbital electronic configuration.
Molecular orbital configuration of the given species is as

\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{CO}(6+8=14)=\sigma 1 s^2, \stackrel{*}{\sigma} 1 s^2, \\
& \sigma 2 s^2, \stackrel{*}{\sigma} 2 s^2, \pi 2 p_x^2 \approx \pi 2 p_y^2, \sigma 2 p_z^2
\end{aligned}

(All the electrons are paired so it is diamagneitc.)

\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{O}_2^{-}(8+8+1=17) \\
& =\sigma 1 s^2, \stackrel{*}{\sigma} 1 s^2, \sigma 2 s^2, \stackrel{*}{\sigma} 2 s^2, \sigma 2 p_z^2, \pi 2 p_x^2 \\
& \approx \pi 2 p_y^2, \stackrel{*}{\pi} 2 p_x^2 \approx \stackrel{*}{\pi} 2 p_y^1
\end{aligned}

It contains one unpaired electron so it is paramagnetic.

\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{CN}^{-}(6+7+1=14)=\text { same as } \mathrm{CO} \\
& \mathrm{NO}^{+}(7+8-1)=\text { same as } \mathrm{CO}
\end{aligned}

Thus, among the given species only $\mathrm{O}_2^{-}$is paramagnetic.

\begin{array}{ll}
\mathrm{NO}_2^{-} & \rightarrow s p^2 \\
\mathrm{NO}_3^{-} & \rightarrow s p^2 \\
\mathrm{NH}_2^{+} & \rightarrow s p^3
\end{array}

\begin{array}{ll}
\mathrm{NH}_4^{-} & \rightarrow s p^3 \\
\mathrm{SCN}^{-} & \rightarrow s p
\end{array}

$\therefore \quad \mathrm{NO}_2^{-}$and $\mathrm{NO}_3^{-}$both have the same hybridisation.
